{"record_id": "entry-1415", "entry_number": 1415, "assignment_position": 99, "frozen_commit": "0089ae722b39edb5180b2496b1f35e2f8fe1ca92", "proposal_status": "repaired", "title": "Democrats Renewed Calls for Trump to Testify as Bill Clinton Answered Epstein Questions", "description_medium": "On Feb. 27, 2026, former President Bill Clinton gave a closed-door deposition to the House Oversight Committee about Jeffrey Epstein. The Associated Press reported that the questioning lasted more than six hours and Clinton said he saw no signs of Epstein’s abuse and had done nothing wrong. Committee members said he answered every question. Democrats used the occasion to renew calls for President Donald Trump to testify; Rep. Suhas Subramanyam said lawmakers were questioning the wrong president.", "description_long": "The House Oversight Committee deposed former President Bill Clinton on Feb. 27, 2026, as part of its investigation of Jeffrey Epstein. The Associated Press reported that Clinton’s closed-door testimony lasted more than six hours and that lawmakers said he answered all their questions. In his opening statement, Clinton said he saw no signs of Epstein’s sexual abuse and had done nothing wrong. He also said he had ended contact with Epstein before Epstein’s 2008 guilty plea. Republicans questioned him about his relationship with Epstein, while Democrats renewed calls for President Donald Trump to testify. Rep. Suhas Subramanyam said the committee was “talking to the wrong president.” AP reported that Clinton had not been accused of wrongdoing and that lawmakers planned to review the depositions before turning to other witnesses. AP reported that Clinton had not been accused of wrongdoing and that lawmakers planned to review the depositions before deciding which witnesses to question next.", "corrected_wording": {"title": "Democrats Renewed Calls for Trump to Testify as Bill Clinton Answered Epstein Questions"}, "source_urls": ["https://apnews.com/article/bill-clinton-jeffrey-epstein-deposition-congress-9ea23ac5a5ffd1c7b9511e46308e8b21", "https://apnews.com/live/donald-trump-clintons-epstein-updates-2-27-2026"], "reference_paths": ["data/neon-workbench/external/description-completion/2026-09-30/lightsprint/input.jsonl", "data/neon-workbench/external/description-completion/2026-09-30/finish-2026-10-01/worker-05-repair-input.jsonl"], "source_access_notes": [], "blockers": [], "rationale": "The sources support the deposition and Democrats’ renewed call for Trump to testify, not the prior assertion that a Trump credibility problem persisted because of the hearing.", "word_counts": {"description_medium": 77, "description_long": 154}, "supersedes_file": "rescue-work/worker-01/resume-20261002-amendment-004.jsonl", "revision_reason": "Cross-batch prose review: removed audit-style or original-claim commentary from reader copy and retained event facts; tracker partials now describe the AP project while specific evidence gaps remain in blockers."}
{"record_id": "entry-1448", "entry_number": 1448, "assignment_position": 113, "frozen_commit": "0089ae722b39edb5180b2496b1f35e2f8fe1ca92", "proposal_status": "repaired", "title": "Trump Ordered Strikes on Iran About Three Hours After Criticizing Nuclear Talks", "description_medium": "On Feb. 27, 2026, Trump said he was unhappy with Iran nuclear negotiations but had not made a final decision. About three hours later, aboard Air Force One, he ordered Operation Epic Fury. The Associated Press reported that he discussed the choice with Republican lawmakers on the flight. Oman’s foreign minister later said a deal had been within reach if diplomacy continued.", "description_long": "The Associated Press timeline places Trump’s public criticism of Iran’s nuclear talks and his order for military action on the same afternoon, Feb. 27, 2026. At 12:25 p.m., he said he was unhappy with the negotiations but had not made a final decision. At 3:38 p.m., while flying to Texas, he directed the launch of Operation Epic Fury. During the flight, he asked a small group of Republican lawmakers for feedback; they discussed continuing talks versus striking, with the participants generally viewing negotiations as delay tactics, according to a person familiar with the discussion. Oman’s foreign minister, who had helped mediate, later said a deal had been within reach and expressed dismay that diplomacy was interrupted. After landing, Trump did not tell reporters he had approved the operation. Oman’s foreign minister later said a nuclear deal with Iran had been within reach if diplomacy continued.", "corrected_wording": {"title": "Trump Ordered Strikes on Iran About Three Hours After Criticizing Nuclear Talks", "original_title": "Trump's Iran Escalation Runway Was Built Through Repeated Public Doubt About Talks", "reason": "The revised description gives the timeline and mediator’s contrasting assessment; it does not infer that Trump intentionally prepared the public for military action."}, "source_urls": ["https://apnews.com/article/6c602da7d44cb8c34fa1a9f85f352e4a", "https://infonews.ca/news/7557218/from-doubts-about-nuke-talks-to-an-air-force-one-flight-what-led-up-to-trumps-order-to-strike-iran/"], "reference_paths": ["data/neon-workbench/external/description-completion/2026-09-30/finish-2026-10-01/worker-09-repair-input.jsonl"], "source_access_notes": ["The AP link did not provide readable article text; the same Associated Press timeline was read in its iNFOnews republication."], "blockers": [], "rationale": "The revised description gives the timeline and mediator’s contrasting assessment; it does not infer that Trump intentionally prepared the public for military action.", "word_counts": {"description_medium": 62, "description_long": 145}, "supersedes_file": "rescue-work/worker-01/resume-20261002-amendment-004.jsonl", "revision_reason": "Cross-batch prose review: removed audit-style or original-claim commentary from reader copy and retained event facts; tracker partials now describe the AP project while specific evidence gaps remain in blockers."}
{"record_id": "entry-1467", "entry_number": 1467, "assignment_position": 123, "frozen_commit": "0089ae722b39edb5180b2496b1f35e2f8fe1ca92", "proposal_status": "repaired", "title": "AP Reported at Least 16 Epstein-Related Files Missing From the DOJ Website", "description_medium": "The Associated Press reported on Dec. 20, 2025, that at least 16 items had disappeared from the Justice Department’s public webpage for Epstein-related records within a day of publication. The missing items included images and a photograph showing Donald Trump, Jeffrey Epstein, Melania Trump and Ghislaine Maxwell. DOJ later said it was reviewing material and redacting files to protect victims; Deputy Attorney General Todd Blanche said prosecutors had flagged material for additional review. The report documents a public-access gap but does not establish who ordered removals or why.", "description_long": "On Dec. 20, 2025, the Associated Press reported that at least 16 items had disappeared from the Justice Department webpage for Epstein-related records less than a day after they were posted. The removed items included images of paintings and a photograph showing Trump with Epstein, Melania Trump and Ghislaine Maxwell. The department initially offered no specific explanation. It later said items were being reviewed and redacted to comply with law and protect victims, while Deputy Attorney General Todd Blanche said prosecutors in New York had flagged material for further review. The report concerns what visitors could see on the public page; it does not show that the underlying government records were destroyed. Nor does it establish who authorized the changes, whether the photograph was removed because of its subjects, or whether a political motive was involved. The department said its review and redactions would continue as it received additional information.", "corrected_wording": {"title": "AP Reported at Least 16 Epstein-Related Files Missing From the DOJ Website", "original_title": "Trump's Full-Disclosure Narrative Was Weakened by AP's DOJ Missing-File Findings", "reason": "The reported count and next-day restoration support an account of public-page changes; the sources do not connect them to a specific Trump promise or establish political motive."}, "source_urls": ["https://apnews.com/article/9290fcaad1cb6fcb1cbc1befabc01994", "https://www.reuters.com/world/us/justice-department-restores-trump-photo-public-database-epstein-files-2025-12-21/", "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/dec/21/epstein-files-photos-removed"], "reference_paths": ["data/neon-workbench/external/description-completion/2026-09-30/finish-2026-10-01/worker-07-repair-input.jsonl"], "source_access_notes": [], "blockers": [], "rationale": "The reported count and next-day restoration support an account of public-page changes; the sources do not connect them to a specific Trump promise or establish political motive.", "word_counts": {"description_medium": 88, "description_long": 150}, "supersedes_file": "rescue-work/worker-01/resume-20261002-amendment-004.jsonl", "revision_reason": "Cross-batch prose review: removed audit-style or original-claim commentary from reader copy and retained event facts; tracker partials now describe the AP project while specific evidence gaps remain in blockers."}
{"record_id": "entry-1471", "entry_number": 1471, "assignment_position": 125, "frozen_commit": "0089ae722b39edb5180b2496b1f35e2f8fe1ca92", "proposal_status": "repaired", "title": "AP Timeline Traced Trump’s Comments, Lawmaker Discussion and Order to Strike Iran", "description_medium": "On Feb. 27, 2026, Trump told reporters he was unhappy with Iran nuclear talks but had not made a final decision. Three hours later, aboard Air Force One, he ordered Operation Epic Fury. AP reported that he consulted Republican lawmakers on the flight about whether to continue diplomacy or strike. Trump spoke to reporters after landing without saying the operation had been approved. Strikes began early the next day.", "description_long": "The Associated Press reconstructed the hours before Trump ordered U.S. strikes on Iran on Feb. 27, 2026. At 12:25 p.m., Trump said he was dissatisfied with nuclear talks but had not made a final decision. At 3:38 p.m., during a flight to Texas, he authorized Operation Epic Fury. AP reported that he asked a small group of Republican lawmakers for feedback, and that they discussed continuing negotiations or proceeding with a strike. Trump spoke to reporters again after landing without saying that an operation had been approved. The first attacks began at 1:15 a.m. the following day, according to the timeline. Gen. Dan Caine said Trump’s order set final military preparations in motion, including readiness checks by air-defense batteries and strike crews. AP later reported that Sen. John Cornyn said Trump had not told lawmakers what he would do. Trump also declined to tell reporters in Texas how close he was to a decision.", "corrected_wording": {"title": "AP Timeline Traced Trump’s Comments, Lawmaker Discussion and Order to Strike Iran", "original_title": "Trump Turned Negotiation Ambiguity Into an Escalation Lever Before Iran Strikes", "reason": "The chronology supports a rapid sequence and a discussion with lawmakers, not the original title’s claim that ambiguity was deliberately used as an escalation lever."}, "source_urls": ["https://apnews.com/article/6c602da7d44cb8c34fa1a9f85f352e4a", "https://infonews.ca/news/7557218/from-doubts-about-nuke-talks-to-an-air-force-one-flight-what-led-up-to-trumps-order-to-strike-iran/"], "reference_paths": ["data/neon-workbench/external/description-completion/2026-09-30/lightsprint/input.jsonl", "data/neon-workbench/external/description-completion/2026-09-30/finish-2026-10-01/worker-09-repair-input.jsonl"], "source_access_notes": ["The AP link did not provide readable article text; the same Associated Press timeline was read in its iNFOnews republication."], "blockers": [], "rationale": "The chronology supports a rapid sequence and a discussion with lawmakers, not the original title’s claim that ambiguity was deliberately used as an escalation lever.", "word_counts": {"description_medium": 69, "description_long": 154}, "supersedes_file": "rescue-work/worker-01/resume-20261002-amendment-004.jsonl", "revision_reason": "Cross-batch prose review: removed audit-style or original-claim commentary from reader copy and retained event facts; tracker partials now describe the AP project while specific evidence gaps remain in blockers."}
{"record_id": "entry-1478", "entry_number": 1478, "assignment_position": 129, "frozen_commit": "0089ae722b39edb5180b2496b1f35e2f8fe1ca92", "proposal_status": "repaired", "title": "AP Compared Trump Administration Statements on Iran Before the 2026 Strikes", "description_medium": "An Associated Press comparison published March 2, 2026, traced changing U.S. statements about Iran. After the June 2025 strikes, President Donald Trump and Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth said Iran’s nuclear sites or weapons capability had been destroyed. Before the February 2026 strikes, Trump and other officials again described Iran as an imminent nuclear threat. The article also compared shifts in statements on regime change and threats to protect Iranian protesters.", "description_long": "The Associated Press reviewed statements by President Donald Trump and administration officials before and after U.S. strikes on Iran. Following the June 2025 attacks, Trump, Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth and the White House said Iran’s nuclear facilities or ability to build a weapon had been destroyed or set back for years. Yet in the run-up to U.S.-Israeli strikes on Feb. 28, 2026, Trump and other officials again described Iran’s nuclear program as an urgent threat. The article also compared changing language on regime change: Trump had endorsed the possibility in a 2025 post, while Vice President JD Vance said the administration did not seek regime change; after the new strikes, Trump urged Iranians to take control of their country, while Hegseth said the operation was not a regime-change war. The report separately traced Trump’s January threats to intervene if Iranian authorities killed protesters and his later comments that killings and planned executions had stopped. ", "corrected_wording": {"title": "AP Compared Trump Administration Statements on Iran Before the 2026 Strikes", "original_title": "Trump's Contradictory Iran Language Increased Miscalculation Risk Across Allies and Adversaries", "reason": "Replaces speculative claims about allied or adversary miscalculation with the documented subjects and contrasts in AP’s statement chronology."}, "source_urls": ["https://apnews.com/article/5357243212b4b8bbd387ae91ca797325", "https://halifax.citynews.ca/2026/03/02/in-their-words-how-trumps-and-his-administrations-statements-on-iran-evolved-and-conflicted/"], "reference_paths": ["data/neon-workbench/external/description-completion/2026-09-30/chatgpt-web/input.jsonl", "data/neon-workbench/external/description-completion/2026-09-30/finish-2026-10-01/worker-01-repair-input.jsonl"], "source_access_notes": [], "blockers": [], "rationale": "Replaces speculative claims about allied or adversary miscalculation with the documented subjects and contrasts in AP’s statement chronology.", "word_counts": {"description_medium": 70, "description_long": 154}, "supersedes_file": "rescue-work/worker-01/resume-20261002-amendment-004.jsonl", "revision_reason": "Cross-batch prose review: removed audit-style or original-claim commentary from reader copy and retained event facts; tracker partials now describe the AP project while specific evidence gaps remain in blockers."}
{"record_id": "entry-1495", "entry_number": 1495, "assignment_position": 137, "frozen_commit": "0089ae722b39edb5180b2496b1f35e2f8fe1ca92", "proposal_status": "repaired", "title": "AP Reported 16 Epstein-Related Files Missing From DOJ Webpage", "description_medium": "The Associated Press reported that 16 files had disappeared from the Justice Department’s public Epstein-records webpage on Dec. 21, 2025. One was a photograph showing Donald Trump with Jeffrey Epstein, Melania Trump and Ghislaine Maxwell. The photograph was later restored after prosecutors reviewed it and found no victim depicted. The report documented a change in what the webpage displayed; it did not establish that the files’ disappearance resulted from a deliberate transparency policy.", "description_long": "The Associated Press reported on Dec. 21, 2025, that 16 files were no longer available on the Justice Department’s webpage for Epstein-related documents. Among them was a photograph showing Donald Trump, Jeffrey Epstein, Melania Trump and Ghislaine Maxwell. The image showed an open desk drawer in Epstein’s New York home with other photographs, including one of Trump. The Justice Department later restored the photograph after New York prosecutors reviewed it and concluded that no victim was depicted; Deputy Attorney General Todd Blanche said the restoration was not prompted by a desire to shield Trump. The episode concerned an observable change to the document portal and a subsequent review of the image. It did not, on its own, show that the administration removed the files to avoid disclosure or measure the overall accuracy of its transparency claims. The source reported no complete explanation for the status of every item in the 16-file count. ", "corrected_wording": {"title": "AP Reported 16 Epstein-Related Files Missing From DOJ Webpage", "original_title": "Trump's Epstein-Era Transparency Claims Took a Structural Hit Once Missing Files Became Publicly Trackable", "reason": "Limits the entry to AP’s documented disappearance report and the later official explanation for restoring the photograph; the larger transparency judgment was unsupported."}, "source_urls": ["https://apnews.com/article/9290fcaad1cb6fcb1cbc1befabc01994", "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/dec/21/epstein-files-photos-removed", "https://www.reuters.com/world/us/justice-department-restores-trump-photo-public-database-epstein-files-2025-12-21/"], "reference_paths": ["data/neon-workbench/external/description-completion/2026-09-30/chatgpt-web/input.jsonl", "data/neon-workbench/external/description-completion/2026-09-30/finish-2026-10-01/worker-09-repair-input.jsonl"], "source_access_notes": ["The AP page did not expose article text in this check; the same-day Guardian report covered the removal and restoration, and Reuters reported the department’s review rationale.", "Read the Guardian’s same-event report for the photograph contents and DOJ restoration."], "blockers": [], "rationale": "Limits the entry to AP’s documented disappearance report and the later official explanation for restoring the photograph; the larger transparency judgment was unsupported.", "word_counts": {"description_medium": 73, "description_long": 152}, "supersedes_file": "rescue-work/worker-01/resume-20261002-amendment-004.jsonl", "revision_reason": "Cross-batch prose review: removed audit-style or original-claim commentary from reader copy and retained event facts; tracker partials now describe the AP project while specific evidence gaps remain in blockers."}
{"record_id": "entry-1502", "entry_number": 1502, "assignment_position": 139, "frozen_commit": "0089ae722b39edb5180b2496b1f35e2f8fe1ca92", "proposal_status": "repaired", "title": "The Guardian Listed Seven Public Episodes That Drew Questions About Trump’s Conduct", "description_medium": "A Jan. 23, 2026 Guardian feature collected seven episodes from Donald Trump’s second term that the newspaper described as confusing or concerning. Examples included Trump appearing to fall asleep during meetings, recounting a false story linking his uncle to Ted Kaczynski, and making unsupported claims about wind turbines and whales. The piece also described Trump confusing Greenland with Iceland and a White House response disputing what viewers had seen. It was a curated commentary feature, not a medical assessment.", "description_long": "The Guardian’s Jan. 23, 2026 feature assembled seven public episodes from Donald Trump’s first year back in office that its writer characterized as bizarre or perplexing. Among them: Trump appeared to close his eyes and nod during government meetings; he told a story suggesting his uncle had taught Ted Kaczynski, although his uncle died before Kaczynski became publicly known as the Unabomber and Kaczynski did not study at MIT; and Trump made unsupported claims that wind turbines drive whales “loco.” The article also recounted Trump confusing Greenland and Iceland in remarks at Davos, followed by press secretary Karoline Leavitt telling a reporter that he had not done so. These examples show the conduct and the White House’s public response that the feature selected for discussion. The article notes that psychiatrists and political opponents have expressed concerns, while White House representatives have praised Trump’s energy and mental sharpness. It offers no clinical diagnosis or formal evaluation. The feature included no clinical diagnosis or formal assessment; White House representatives praised Trump’s energy and mental sharpness.", "corrected_wording": {"title": "The Guardian Listed Seven Public Episodes That Drew Questions About Trump’s Conduct", "original_title": "Trump's Public Conduct Pattern Fed a Broader Competence Crisis in the Presidency", "reason": "Replaces a generalized competence-crisis assertion with examples reported in the Guardian feature and clarifies that it did not offer a clinical assessment."}, "source_urls": ["https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/jan/23/trump-concerning-moments"], "reference_paths": ["data/neon-workbench/external/description-completion/2026-09-30/finish-2026-10-01/worker-12-repair-input.jsonl"], "source_access_notes": [], "blockers": [], "rationale": "Replaces a generalized competence-crisis assertion with examples reported in the Guardian feature and clarifies that it did not offer a clinical assessment.", "word_counts": {"description_medium": 79, "description_long": 173}, "supersedes_file": "rescue-work/worker-01/resume-20261002-amendment-004.jsonl", "revision_reason": "Cross-batch prose review: removed audit-style or original-claim commentary from reader copy and retained event facts; tracker partials now describe the AP project while specific evidence gaps remain in blockers."}
{"record_id": "entry-1517", "entry_number": 1517, "assignment_position": 145, "frozen_commit": "0089ae722b39edb5180b2496b1f35e2f8fe1ca92", "proposal_status": "repaired", "title": "Thomas Massie Said Iran Strikes Would Not Make Epstein Files Disappear", "description_medium": "On March 1, 2026, Republican Rep. Thomas Massie posted that bombing Iran would not make the Epstein files go away. The Independent reported his comment after U.S. and Israeli strikes on Iran began and described other criticism from Marjorie Taylor Greene and Sen. Rand Paul. The article presented Massie’s statement as a political criticism of the military action; it did not provide evidence that the strikes were ordered to distract from the files.", "description_long": "After U.S. and Israeli strikes on Iran began on Feb. 28, 2026, Kentucky Republican Rep. Thomas Massie wrote on X that bombing a country would not make the Epstein files go away. The Independent reported the post on March 2 and noted that the strikes had displaced the Epstein controversy from the immediate news agenda. Massie had helped lead passage of the Epstein Files Transparency Act. The article also described criticism from former Rep. Marjorie Taylor Greene, who objected to the overseas military action in light of the administration’s handling of the files, and Sen. Rand Paul, who argued that Congress holds the constitutional power to initiate war. These were lawmakers’ statements and political interpretations. The article did not establish that the Iran operation was undertaken to divert attention from Epstein-related disclosures, nor that public trust in national-security claims measurably changed because the issues overlapped in the news. ", "corrected_wording": {"title": "Thomas Massie Said Iran Strikes Would Not Make Epstein Files Disappear", "original_title": "Trump's Iran-Epstein Collision Narrative Magnified Credibility Strain on National-Security Claims", "reason": "Attributes the distraction claim to Rep. Massie and other critics rather than presenting it as an established motive or measured credibility effect."}, "source_urls": ["https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/us-politics/trump-iran-epstein-files-massie-b2930178.html"], "reference_paths": ["data/neon-workbench/external/description-completion/2026-09-30/chatgpt-web/input.jsonl", "data/neon-workbench/external/description-completion/2026-09-30/finish-2026-10-01/worker-08-repair-input.jsonl"], "source_access_notes": [], "blockers": [], "rationale": "Attributes the distraction claim to Rep. Massie and other critics rather than presenting it as an established motive or measured credibility effect.", "word_counts": {"description_medium": 73, "description_long": 148}, "supersedes_file": "rescue-work/worker-01/resume-20261002-amendment-004.jsonl", "revision_reason": "Cross-batch prose review: removed audit-style or original-claim commentary from reader copy and retained event facts; tracker partials now describe the AP project while specific evidence gaps remain in blockers."}
{"record_id": "entry-1521", "entry_number": 1521, "assignment_position": 147, "frozen_commit": "0089ae722b39edb5180b2496b1f35e2f8fe1ca92", "proposal_status": "supported_partial", "title": "AP Maintained a Tracker of Trump’s Campaign Promises", "description_medium": "The Associated Press’s online project tracks selected promises Donald Trump made during his campaign. It presents individual commitments and reported progress over time.", "description_long": "AP’s campaign-promise tracker follows selected commitments Donald Trump made during his campaign and reports their progress individually. It offers updates on pledges across policy areas, allowing readers to follow reported implementation. The project covers selected promises rather than every campaign pledge, and its listed statuses are updates on particular commitments rather than an overall score of Trump’s performance.", "corrected_wording": {"title": "AP Maintained a Tracker of Trump’s Campaign Promises", "original_title": "Trump's Promise Tracker Gap Showed Delivery Claims Outrunning Documented Outcomes", "reason": "The live project page exposed no dated rows or status snapshot; the description limits itself to the tracker’s existence instead of claiming a quantified performance gap."}, "source_urls": ["https://apnews.com/projects/trump-campaign-promise-tracker/"], "reference_paths": ["data/neon-workbench/external/description-completion/2026-09-30/chatgpt-web/input.jsonl", "data/neon-workbench/external/description-completion/2026-09-30/finish-2026-10-01/worker-10-repair-input.jsonl"], "source_access_notes": ["The AP tracker landing page exposed no dated pledge rows or status snapshot."], "blockers": ["The AP project landing page exposed no dated snapshot, promise-level rows, or status definitions for March 3, 2026. The record also names no Trump statement asserting complete delivery. Those facts are necessary to test the claimed gap between delivery rhetoric and outcomes."], "rationale": "The tracker landing 