{"record_id": "entry-1648", "entry_number": 1648, "assignment_position": 211, "frozen_commit": "0089ae722b39edb5180b2496b1f35e2f8fe1ca92", "proposal_status": "repaired", "title": "The Atlantic’s “Unthinkable” Project Ranked 50 Episodes From Trump’s First Term", "description_medium": "The Atlantic’s “Unthinkable” project assembled and subjectively ranked 50 incidents from Donald Trump’s first presidency. Its introduction said the list covered norm-bending and destructive events through the halfway point of his term. Examples included Trump’s praise of a congressman who body-slammed a reporter, the firing of James Comey, Trump’s call to pardon himself, and children separated from parents. The project was a retrospective editorial list, not an analysis of a later crisis.", "description_long": "The Atlantic’s “Unthinkable” page presents a retrospective project titled “50 Moments That Define an Improbable Presidency.” Editor in chief Jeffrey Goldberg wrote that the magazine selected 50 incidents from the first half of Trump’s first term, then ranked them subjectively for both outlandishness and importance. The entries include Trump praising a congressman who body-slammed a reporter, threatening to remove security clearances from critics, the firing of FBI Director James Comey, and children being separated from their parents and incarcerated. The introduction says the magazine regarded each listed moment as something that would have been unthinkable in most previous presidencies. The project is an editorial framing of selected events from 2017–2018. It links each ranked incident to a separate essay. The selected incidents date from 2017 and 2018 and span election disputes, foreign policy, press freedom, immigration and pressure on public officials.", "corrected_wording": {"title": "The Atlantic’s “Unthinkable” Project Ranked 50 Episodes From Trump’s First Term", "original_title": "Trump's Crisis Politics Was Framed as Expanding the Boundaries of Acceptable Abuse", "reason": "Corrects the date and scope to describe the Atlantic’s 2019 first-term retrospective, rather than asserting an unlinked 2026 crisis effect."}, "source_urls": ["https://www.theatlantic.com/unthinkable/"], "reference_paths": ["data/neon-workbench/external/description-completion/2026-09-30/lightsprint/input.jsonl", "data/neon-workbench/external/description-completion/2026-09-30/finish-2026-10-01/worker-06-repair-input.jsonl"], "source_access_notes": [], "blockers": [], "rationale": "Corrects the date and scope to describe the Atlantic’s 2019 first-term retrospective, rather than asserting an unlinked 2026 crisis effect.", "word_counts": {"description_medium": 72, "description_long": 141}, "supersedes_file": "rescue-work/worker-01/resume-20261002-amendment-004.jsonl", "revision_reason": "Cross-batch prose review: removed audit-style or original-claim commentary from reader copy and retained event facts; tracker partials now describe the AP project while specific evidence gaps remain in blockers."}
{"record_id": "entry-1650", "entry_number": 1650, "assignment_position": 213, "frozen_commit": "0089ae722b39edb5180b2496b1f35e2f8fe1ca92", "proposal_status": "repaired", "title": "The Atlantic Ranked 50 “Unthinkable” Moments From Trump’s First Term", "description_medium": "In a project published midway through Trump’s first term, The Atlantic selected and subjectively ranked 50 events it called improbable, norm-bending or destructive. The list included Trump’s election complaints, the firing of James Comey, threats to strip critics’ security clearances and the separation of children from parents.", "description_long": "The Atlantic’s “Unthinkable” project catalogued 50 moments from Donald Trump’s first term and ranked them, in the magazine’s words, subjectively by outlandishness and importance. Editor in chief Jeffrey Goldberg introduced the list at the halfway point of Trump’s first term, describing its entries as improbable, norm-bending and destructive incidents. Examples ranged from Trump’s complaint about an election he won to threats to strip critics’ security clearances, the firing of James Comey and the separation of children from parents. The project reflected the magazine’s view that such incidents would have been unthinkable in most prior presidencies. Its entries range from election disputes to foreign policy, press freedom and the treatment of immigrants. The selected incidents date from 2017 and 2018 and span election disputes, foreign policy, press freedom, immigration and pressure on public officials. Editors ranked the moments by both outlandishness and importance, and linked each to a separate essay.", "corrected_wording": {"title": "The Atlantic Ranked 50 “Unthinkable” Moments From Trump’s First Term", "original_title": "Trump's 'Unthinkable' Arc Reinforced Calls for Stronger Democratic Guardrails", "reason": "Removes the unsupported guardrail-effect claim and identifies the retrospective project and its actual time period."}, "source_urls": ["https://www.theatlantic.com/unthinkable/"], "reference_paths": ["data/neon-workbench/external/description-completion/2026-09-30/finish-2026-10-01/worker-08-repair-input.jsonl"], "source_access_notes": [], "blockers": [], "rationale": "Removes the unsupported guardrail-effect claim and identifies the retrospective project and its actual time period.", "word_counts": {"description_medium": 47, "description_long": 149}, "supersedes_file": "rescue-work/worker-01/resume-20261002-amendment-004.jsonl", "revision_reason": "Cross-batch prose review: removed audit-style or original-claim commentary from reader copy and retained event facts; tracker partials now describe the AP project while specific evidence gaps remain in blockers."}
{"record_id": "entry-1662", "entry_number": 1662, "assignment_position": 221, "frozen_commit": "0089ae722b39edb5180b2496b1f35e2f8fe1ca92", "proposal_status": "repaired", "title": "NPR Reported That Only One of Four 2019 FBI Interviews Was Publicly Available", "description_medium": "NPR reported that FBI records showed four 2019 interviews with a woman who alleged abuse involving Trump and Jeffrey Epstein, but only the first interview appeared in DOJ’s public database. That first report did not mention Trump. NPR’s review of serial numbers and Maxwell-case logs indicated that 53 pages of interview documents and notes appeared missing. The allegation remains unverified; DOJ declined to explain the specific files.", "description_long": "NPR’s Feb. 24 investigation compared the Justice Department’s public Epstein-files database with FBI records, emails and discovery logs from Ghislaine Maxwell’s criminal case. NPR found references to four FBI interviews with a woman who alleged that Donald Trump abused her when she was a minor, but only the first report was publicly available; it did not mention Trump. Serial numbers and case logs suggested 53 pages of interview documents and notes were missing from the public database. NPR also reported that an FBI list and a Justice Department slideshow contained an unverified allegation, and that DOJ declined to explain the specific missing pages. The White House denied wrongdoing by Trump. The report did not establish that the allegation was true or that Trump directed the handling of the files. The other referenced interview summaries and notes were not available in the public database when NPR compared the records.", "corrected_wording": {"title": "NPR Reported That Only One of Four 2019 FBI Interviews Was Publicly Available", "original_title": "Trump's Epstein Defense Continued to Face Chronology-Based Consistency Pressure", "reason": "Replaces an unsupported consistency-effect claim with NPR’s specific interview chronology and record comparison."}, "source_urls": ["https://www.npr.org/2026/02/24/nx-s1-5723968/epstein-files-trump-accusation-maxwell", "https://www.lpm.org/news/2026-02-24/justice-department-withheld-and-removed-some-epstein-files-related-to-trump"], "reference_paths": ["data/neon-workbench/external/description-completion/2026-09-30/chatgpt-web/input.jsonl", "data/neon-workbench/external/description-completion/2026-09-30/finish-2026-10-01/worker-04-repair-input.jsonl"], "source_access_notes": ["Read NPR’s investigation through Louisville Public Media’s NPR republication."], "blockers": [], "rationale": "Replaces an unsupported consistency-effect claim with NPR’s specific interview chronology and record comparison.", "word_counts": {"description_medium": 67, "description_long": 148}, "supersedes_file": "rescue-work/worker-01/resume-20261002-amendment-004.jsonl", "revision_reason": "Cross-batch prose review: removed audit-style or original-claim commentary from reader copy and retained event facts; tracker partials now describe the AP project while specific evidence gaps remain in blockers."}
