{"record_id": "entry-1671", "entry_number": 1671, "assignment_position": 227, "frozen_commit": "0089ae722b39edb5180b2496b1f35e2f8fe1ca92", "proposal_status": "repaired", "title": "AP Timeline Showed Trump’s Strike Order Followed Comments on Iran Talks and a Flight Consultation", "description_medium": "An AP timeline reports that Trump told reporters on Feb. 27, 2026, that he was unhappy with Iran nuclear talks and had made no final decision. About three hours later, he approved Operation Epic Fury aboard Air Force One after asking Republican lawmakers about negotiations versus a strike. AP also reported officials told congressional staff that U.S. intelligence did not indicate Iran was preparing a preemptive attack.", "description_long": "The Associated Press timeline of the Iran operation shows President Donald Trump’s public statements, private consultation and authorization in sequence. At 12:25 p.m. Eastern on Feb. 27, he said he was unhappy with nuclear negotiations and had not made a final decision. At 3:38 p.m., aboard Air Force One to Texas, he approved Operation Epic Fury. During the flight he asked Republican lawmakers whether to continue diplomacy or strike; an unnamed person familiar with the discussion said those present generally viewed talks as a delay tactic. The operation began at 1:15 a.m. on Feb. 28. AP also reported that administration officials told congressional staff on March 1 that U.S. intelligence did not indicate Iran was preparing a preemptive strike against the United States; they cited a broader regional threat from Iran and proxy forces instead. On March 2, Hegseth said the operation would not become a long, sustained conflict; Trump said it could last four or five weeks or longer.", "corrected_wording": {"title": "AP Timeline Showed Trump’s Strike Order Followed Comments on Iran Talks and a Flight Consultation", "original_title": "Trump's Iran Decision Chain Continued to Prioritize Acceleration Over Deliberation", "reason": "Corrects 